  "text": "In this study, we observed a significant positive correlation between ABSI and diabetes (Fig 2 and Table 2). Each unit increase in ABSI was associated with 42% to 66% higher odds of diabetes across multiply adjusted models, and participants in the highest ABSI quartile had nearly 96% higher odds than those in the lowest quartile, supporting ABSI as an independent marker of diabetes risk. These findings are corroborated by previous studies. In the Qatari population, ABSI predicted diabetes risk more strongly than BMI; Bawadi et al. (2019) reported an adjusted odds ratio of 1.85 (95% CI: 1.54\u20132.23) per 1-SD increment in ABSI versus 1.34 (95% CI: 1.18\u20131.51) for BMI among 2,536 adults [28]. A rural Chinese study also found a significant association, although ABSI showed lower predictive accuracy than WHtR and BRI (AUC 0.61 in both sexes) [29]. In two Japanese adult cohorts, multivariable Cox regression likewise identified an association with Type 2 diabetes, with consistent results across population subgroups [30, 31]. Despite differences in study design, population, and statistical approach, the evidence collectively supports ABSI as a relevant and broadly applicable marker of diabetes risk.",
